  "verbalization": "Aztreonam, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Rhinoscleroma through a chain of indications that connect it to Escherichia coli infection and then to Rhinoscleroma. The evidence suggests that Aztreonam is used to treat infections caused by Escherichia coli, which in turn are linked to Rhinoscleroma. This implies a potential mechanistic pathway where Aztreonam may inhibit bacterial growth, thereby reducing the risk of Rhinoscleroma. The presence of a direct indication from Aztreonam to Rhinoscleroma, though indirect, supports the hypothesis that the drug may have a role in the treatment of this condition. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, suggesting that further mechanistic validation is warranted. The integration of these paths highlights the importance of targeting bacterial infections as a potential therapeutic strategy for Rhinoscleroma.",
